WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:03.589
We're now going to take a look at some of the optimization

2
00:00:03.589 --> 00:00:05.769
tools Unreal Engine 5 provides to help

3
00:00:05.769 --> 00:00:09.130
you optimize your visuals, as well as the content inside

4
00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.169
of your project.

5
00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:12.960
The first tool that we're going to be taking a look at

6
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.640
is the scalability settings, what they do,

7
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.440
and how you access them.

8
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.440
The scalability system in Unreal

9
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.800
lets you adjust the quality of rendering features

10
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.120
to help your game run smoothly

11
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.539
across different hardware and software platforms.

12
00:00:25.539 --> 00:00:27.079
They allow you to balance performance

13
00:00:27.079 --> 00:00:28.879
and visual fidelity, ensuring players

14
00:00:28.879 --> 00:00:32.759
on both high and low end systems get an optimized experience.

15
00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.619
Essentially, scalability provides a way

16
00:00:34.619 --> 00:00:36.600
to scale your game's graphical quality based

17
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.000
on system capabilities.

18
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.679
You could access scalability settings directly

19
00:00:41.679 --> 00:00:44.240
from the Unreal Editor toolbar, or you

20
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.250
can use the settings inside of your system's I&I.

21
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:50.810
From the toolbar, all we need to do

22
00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:53.609
is come up here to the performance and scalability

23
00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:55.329
tools tied to this viewport, click,

24
00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:57.409
and then we see our viewport scalability.

25
00:00:57.409 --> 00:01:03.210
In this menu right here, we can select either high, medium,

26
00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:08.459
or low, or we can actually adjust the individual settings

27
00:01:08.459 --> 00:01:12.310
to get something that works for our particular project.

28
00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:17.379
You can adjust what affects these settings further

29
00:01:17.379 --> 00:01:20.659
by opening up your project's settings I&I file

30
00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:23.219
and then adjusting individual properties that

31
00:01:23.219 --> 00:01:26.159
will be enabled or disabled in each one

32
00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:28.549
of these subsections.

33
00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:30.670
Remember that scalability settings

34
00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.790
are critical to your project's performance,

35
00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:38.000
and they can help you customize each level so

36
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.120
that anyone who plays your project

37
00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.480
across a wide range of hardware

38
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.280
will have a great experience.

39
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.219
Here is an example of scalabilities at work.

40
00:01:50.219 --> 00:01:53.640
We have low all the way over here on the left-hand side,

41
00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:57.760
and then medium, high, epic, and cinematic.

42
00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.519
Now, going from low to medium, it's

43
00:02:00.519 --> 00:02:03.180
pretty easy to see the difference.

44
00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:07.000
We are missing all types of shadowing information on low.

45
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:13.810
There is no subsurface inside of the foliage here.

46
00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.250
There's no atmospheric.

47
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:15.889
There's no fog.

48
00:02:15.889 --> 00:02:17.210
There's no nothing really.

49
00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.129
Going on to medium, what we see is we get our shadows back.

50
00:02:20.129 --> 00:02:23.650
We get a little bit of fog, not a lot of atmosphere,

51
00:02:23.689 --> 00:02:26.250
but definitely something that is giving us

52
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.969
some more options inside of our rendering here.

53
00:02:28.969 --> 00:02:32.050
Once we go over to high, this is where things really

54
00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:33.930
start to look different.

55
00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:37.770
We've got subsurface scattering going on on the trees.

56
00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.050
We have much better global illumination

57
00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.569
across the entire scene.

58
00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:44.789
We have depth of field, god rays, bloom,

59
00:02:44.789 --> 00:02:46.370
lots of stuff going on.

60
00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:49.210
And then moving on to epic and cinematic,

61
00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.270
it's almost impossible for epic and cinematic

62
00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:52.849
to tell the difference between the two.

63
00:02:52.889 --> 00:02:54.889
Basically, what we've done is we've taken everything

64
00:02:54.889 --> 00:02:58.530
from high in epic, and we have turned it up to the maximum.

65
00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.169
And then we've taken that one step further in cinematic

66
00:03:01.169 --> 00:03:05.000
and just turned everything up even further.

67
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.159
Don't forget that every section in the scalability

68
00:03:07.159 --> 00:03:09.240
system is fully customizable, allowing

69
00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.039
you to define exactly which rendering features are enabled

70
00:03:12.039 --> 00:03:17.460
or disabled at each quality level from low to epic.

71
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.099
As I mentioned earlier, in addition

72
00:03:19.099 --> 00:03:21.900
to the editor interface, we can configure our scalability

73
00:03:21.939 --> 00:03:25.460
settings in Blueprints using nodes such as set scalability

74
00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:29.259
level or by adjusting individual settings

75
00:03:29.259 --> 00:03:31.300
at runtime.

76
00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:34.840
We can also use INI files like the default scalability

77
00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:38.280
dot INI or the platform specific configuration

78
00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.189
files such as Android scalability.

79
00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:47.000
These will allow us to automatically apply scalability

80
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.099
settings to any of the platforms

81
00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:51.039
our project is going to run on.

82
00:03:51.039 --> 00:03:52.800
Scalability settings are your main tool

83
00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.759
for balancing performance versus quality

84
00:03:55.759 --> 00:03:58.800
and can help when customizing rendering features

85
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.229
on a per platform basis.

86
00:04:01.229 --> 00:04:03.150
Remember, you can control all of these settings

87
00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.789
via Blueprints or configuration files.

88
00:04:05.789 --> 00:04:07.629
And this is going to be essential for keeping

89
00:04:07.629 --> 00:04:09.590
your game optimized, adaptable,

90
00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:13.360
and running smoothly across every device it supports.

91
00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.360
Device profiles are a tool that

92
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:16.879
allow you to fine tune performance

93
00:04:16.879 --> 00:04:20.079
settings for specific platforms and hardware types.

94
00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:23.240
It is a companion system to scalability settings

95
00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.040
and allows you more precise control

96
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.730
over how your game behaves on different devices.

97
00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.129
You can use device profiles to override or adjust

98
00:04:31.129 --> 00:04:33.370
scalability settings and many other parameters

99
00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:37.519
to make sure your game runs optimally on every platform.

100
00:04:37.519 --> 00:04:40.879
To access device profiles, we come up to,

101
00:04:40.879 --> 00:04:44.199
inside of the editor, we go to our tools,

102
00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:48.480
we go to our platform, and we go to device profiles.

103
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.879
We will need to give this just a second to configure itself.

104
00:04:52.879 --> 00:04:53.800
There we go.

105
00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:55.920
And once we have done that, we can see

106
00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.839
these are all the different device profiles

107
00:04:57.839 --> 00:05:00.870
that come default with the editor.

108
00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.470
Let's look at the Windows no editor.

109
00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:05.509
We can click on these three little dots right here

110
00:05:05.509 --> 00:05:08.430
to open this up in its own editing window.

111
00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.310
And then once we have this, we can see

112
00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.350
all of the different settings that are inside of here.

113
00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.430
So for example, we have all of our texture settings.

114
00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:18.110
If we want to add something, for example,

115
00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:19.430
we can come up here to rendering

116
00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:22.990
and we could say allow.hdr and we could set this to one,

117
00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:27.990
which means that when we use the Windows no editor,

118
00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:32.040
we are going to allow HDR support.

119
00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:35.680
We can look for all types of different parameters

120
00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.040
to enable or disable.

121
00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:40.199
And again, what this does is this allows us

122
00:05:40.199 --> 00:05:44.120
to fine tune our project so that if there is

123
00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.399
a specific setting on a specific chipset

124
00:05:46.399 --> 00:05:48.480
or a specific device that doesn't work,

125
00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.879
we have the ability to counteract that setting

126
00:05:50.879 --> 00:05:54.800
here inside of the device profiles.

127
00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.800
Remember, we can also adjust other parameters

128
00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.839
such as which scalability level the device will use,

129
00:06:01.839 --> 00:06:03.519
rendering and texture settings,

130
00:06:03.519 --> 00:06:06.879
performance overrides for resolution or anti-aliasing

131
00:06:06.879 --> 00:06:09.000
and even platform specific optimizations

132
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.439
for memory and GPU usage.

133
00:06:11.439 --> 00:06:14.790
This tool, the device profiles tool,

134
00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:18.350
really allows you to tailor your games experience

135
00:06:18.389 --> 00:06:22.149
for each platform that you are supporting

136
00:06:22.149 --> 00:06:27.629
without having to change anything about the core game logic.

137
