00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:04.360
Today I want to enable virtual texture streaming.

266
00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:06.480
I could just click this little check mark box here

267
00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:08.779
and now I've enabled virtual texture streaming

268
00:12:08.779 --> 00:12:11.840
for all of those textures and there we go.

269
00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:13.200
I have actually disabled it.

270
00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:15.879
So this is a really, really great tool

271
00:12:15.879 --> 00:12:18.299
for doing things just like that.

272
00:12:18.299 --> 00:12:21.139
Now, a couple of things to consider.

273
00:12:21.139 --> 00:12:24.120
With performance, if you selected a large number of assets,

274
00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:27.480
the update process might take a little while

275
00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:28.899
and it may even appear that the editor

276
00:12:28.899 --> 00:12:30.559
has frozen or crashed, but don't worry,

277
00:12:30.559 --> 00:12:32.279
it's still processing it in the background.

278
00:12:32.279 --> 00:12:33.879
Just give it time to complete.

279
00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:38.759
The property matrix is often used for textures,

280
00:12:38.759 --> 00:12:41.320
but it also supports many other asset types

281
00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:42.960
that you can select from the content browser,

282
00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:44.960
such as material, sounds and animation.

283
00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:47.379
So it's a great general purpose tool

284
00:12:47.379 --> 00:12:50.879
for bulk editing across a variety of content.

285
00:12:50.879 --> 00:12:53.139
With that said, you need to make sure

286
00:12:53.139 --> 00:12:56.200
that you select assets that are all of the same type.

287
00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:58.379
So if you're gonna be working with textures,

288
00:12:58.379 --> 00:13:01.519
you need to make sure you have all of textures selected.

289
00:13:01.519 --> 00:13:05.139
If you have, say, a static mesh or a material selected,

290
00:13:05.139 --> 00:13:07.720
you're gonna notice that none of the properties

291
00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:10.039
that you're used to seeing are gonna be there.

292
00:13:10.039 --> 00:13:14.049
And that's, again, because certain options

293
00:13:14.049 --> 00:13:16.169
aren't available across all asset types.

294
00:13:16.169 --> 00:13:19.840
So you need to make sure that you do have

295
00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:22.559
all of the same asset type selected.

296
00:13:22.559 --> 00:13:25.440
There are also not all settings are exposed

297
00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:26.419
to the property matrix.

298
00:13:26.419 --> 00:13:28.759
For example, LOD settings for static meshes

299
00:13:28.759 --> 00:13:30.799
and some advanced configuration options

300
00:13:30.799 --> 00:13:32.440
aren't available through this tool.

301
00:13:33.279 --> 00:13:36.539
So if you need to bulk edit assets with those properties,

302
00:13:36.539 --> 00:13:38.860
you'll need to use editor utility blueprints

303
00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:42.740
or the static mesh editor subsystem.

304
00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:45.820
So remember that we can use bulk editing

305
00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:48.700
for large scale property changes quickly,

306
00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:51.289
efficiently and reliably.

307
00:13:51.289 --> 00:13:54.549
Be patient with large selections.

308
00:13:54.549 --> 00:13:56.389
And remember that you can only operate

309
00:13:56.389 --> 00:13:58.950
on assets of the same type.

310
00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:01.389
So only on materials, only on textures,

311
00:14:01.389 --> 00:14:02.789
only on static meshes.

312
00:14:05.059 --> 00:14:07.860
We're now gonna take a look at packed level instances

313
00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.539
as they are a really useful tool

314
00:14:11.539 --> 00:14:14.500
that allows you to kit bash large scale environments

315
00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:17.980
by assembling them from smaller reusable pieces.

316
00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:21.100
And we can actually see an example of that

317
00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:24.659
in the image that we're seeing right here.

318
00:14:24.659 --> 00:14:28.960
This image on the right hand side was created

319
00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:33.330
using all of these rock sandstone meshes

320
00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:37.289
and various rotations, scales and transforms.

321
00:14:37.289 --> 00:14:39.590
Once we were happy with the overall result,

322
00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:41.370
we selected all of them.

323
00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:43.730
And then from the selection menu,

324
00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:47.429
we selected this packed level instance blueprint.

325
00:14:47.429 --> 00:14:49.690
What this is gonna do is then it's gonna give us

326
00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:53.049
a actor that actually contains all of those meshes

327
00:14:53.049 --> 00:14:55.210
that we can quickly place in any level.

328
00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:57.889
And one of the other added benefits of this

329
00:14:57.889 --> 00:14:59.210
is that it's actually gonna replace

330
00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:01.169
all of the static meshes in there

331
00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:03.769
with instanced static mesh versions,

332
00:15:03.769 --> 00:15:06.730
which makes things run a little bit faster.

333
00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:09.490
Now let's pop over into the editor right now

334
00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:11.529
and kind of have a look at how this works.

335
00:15:11.529 --> 00:15:14.269
So here we have just a simple box set up.

336
00:15:14.269 --> 00:15:16.090
And the reason that I have two of them

337
00:15:16.090 --> 00:15:21.659
is there's actually two versions of this tool.

338
00:15:21.659 --> 00:15:23.899
And the version that you select

339
00:15:23.899 --> 00:15:27.779
depends on what is contained in your selection.

340
00:15:27.779 --> 00:15:31.220
So first off, if we select all of our static mesh,

341
00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.860
anything that's static that doesn't require

342
00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:36.139
say a trigger volume or some type of gameplay

343
00:15:36.139 --> 00:15:38.000
or something like that, if we select all of them,

344
00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.340
we right click and we come down to our level.

345
00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:43.519
This is where we're gonna create a packed level actor.

346
00:15:43.519 --> 00:15:44.940
So we can click on this.

347
00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:47.379
It's gonna ask us where we want the pivot to be placed.

348
00:15:47.379 --> 00:15:49.759
Right now I'm just gonna do the center minimum Z

349
00:15:49.759 --> 00:15:51.059
and press okay.

350
00:15:51.059 --> 00:15:59.159
And we are gonna call this and press save.

351
00:15:59.519 --> 00:16:00.600
And then it's gonna ask us

352
00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:02.559
where we wanna save the blueprint version of this.

353
00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:05.879
And we're gonna go ahead and just let it be to the default.

354
00:16:05.879 --> 00:16:07.360
And what we're gonna get is

355
00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:09.480
this is our packed level blueprint.

356
00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:10.500
Now there's a couple of cool things

357
00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:11.340
that we could do with this.

358
00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:14.019
If we right click on it and we come to the level,

359
00:16:14.019 --> 00:16:15.240
we can edit it.

360
00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:18.559
And then inside of here, we can do things like this.

361
00:16:18.559 --> 00:16:20.639
And if I press save, what's gonna happen

362
00:16:20.639 --> 00:16:22.559
is all of them are gonna be updated.

363
00:16:22.559 --> 00:16:24.679
But the other cool thing that we can do

364
00:16:24.679 --> 00:16:26.820
is these are meant to be nested

365
00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:29.159
inside of other configurations.

366
00:16:29.159 --> 00:16:32.120
So I'm just gonna flip these around like this,

367
00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:35.759
flip all the way around and just move it over like so.

368
00:16:35.759 --> 00:16:36.600
So there we go.

369
00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:39.240
So what we could do too is we can right click on this

370
00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:44.879
and go to level and go create packed level actor.

371
00:16:45.159 --> 00:16:52.029
And then we'll call this one pack two and press save.

372
00:16:52.029 --> 00:16:53.669
And then we'll save this as well.

373
00:16:53.669 --> 00:16:55.850
And now what we've got is, well, that's pack one,

374
00:16:55.850 --> 00:16:57.509
but this is pack two.

375
00:16:57.509 --> 00:17:00.750
And if we, whoops, if we right click on this

376
00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:04.430
and we go to our level, we can actually edit this

377
00:17:05.029 --> 00:17:07.990
and you can see here, we can also right click on this

378
00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:11.049
and go to our level and then edit this one.

379
00:17:11.049 --> 00:17:19.420
Whoops, level and level and edit.

380
00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:22.809
Let's just do that one more time.

381
00:17:22.809 --> 00:17:23.750
There we go.

382
00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:25.869
And now we can edit the individual one.

383
00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:29.230
So I can put something like this and then save

384
00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:32.390
and it's gonna update like it did over there.

385
00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:33.230
So that's really cool.

386
00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:35.789
We can actually nest these so that when we

387
00:17:35.789 --> 00:17:39.849
are editing them, we can make massive edits

388
00:17:39.849 --> 00:17:41.009
across the entire world.

389
00:17:41.009 --> 00:17:43.609
And the other cool thing is if we want to use these,

390
00:17:43.609 --> 00:17:46.150
say as a starting point, we can even come here

391
00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:48.609
to our level and we can break them.

392
00:17:48.609 --> 00:17:51.809
And what that's gonna do is it's gonna break this

393
00:17:51.809 --> 00:17:55.529
in it to its individual components.

394
00:17:55.529 --> 00:17:57.369
Further break these down by just going here

395
00:17:57.369 --> 00:17:59.089
to break and break.

396
00:17:59.089 --> 00:18:00.710
Yes, and there we go.

397
00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:03.970
We've actually broken this down into the individual pieces.

398
00:18:03.970 --> 00:18:07.690
So we can use these again as a great starting point

399
00:18:07.690 --> 00:18:10.849
or once we have, then once we're done,

400
00:18:10.849 --> 00:18:13.730
we can always repack them back into something else.

401
00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:16.529
Now, there is one other version of these.

402
00:18:16.529 --> 00:18:19.490
And if I select all of these objects here,

403
00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:21.130
like so and I get the light and I right click,

404
00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:23.049
we're gonna go to level and we're gonna create

405
00:18:23.049 --> 00:18:25.170
a level instance.

406
00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:27.769
So the difference between the two is that

407
00:18:27.769 --> 00:18:32.769
the level instanced can actually contain things

408
00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:36.170
like blueprints or triggers or lights.

409
00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:39.809
We are gonna call this level instance underscore zero one

410
00:18:39.809 --> 00:18:41.670
and click save.

411
00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:44.849
And again, the major difference is that we are just going

412
00:18:44.849 --> 00:18:46.910
to, if I was to hit control B to look at this,

413
00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:50.210
we are getting this level here that I can pull in,

414
00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:52.730
but we don't actually get a blueprint like we did

415
00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:53.569
in the other one.

416
00:18:53.569 --> 00:18:57.890
And again, remember the reason that we have two of them

417
00:18:57.890 --> 00:19:02.269
or the different versions is that the packed level actor

418
00:19:02.269 --> 00:19:06.289
is for things that are static, that aren't gonna move,

419
00:19:07.109 --> 00:19:08.690
maybe a massive building or a bunch of rocks

420
00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:09.750
or things like that.

421
00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:15.869
The level actor itself is for when we have something

422
00:19:18.049 --> 00:19:22.349
that is gonna require some type of blueprint interaction.

423
00:19:22.349 --> 00:19:24.369
Maybe there's a light or something like that.

424
00:19:24.369 --> 00:19:28.849
So we wanna use that one if we ever want to, again,

425
00:19:28.849 --> 00:19:32.170
have something that contains something else

426
00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:33.849
other than a static mesh.

427
00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:38.980
So which one you use depends on the needs of your project.

428
00:19:40.779 --> 00:19:43.339
You can use a mix of both of them.

429
00:19:43.339 --> 00:19:46.700
You can use just primarily level packed actors,

430
00:19:46.700 --> 00:19:49.579
or you can use primarily level instance.

431
00:19:49.579 --> 00:19:52.460
It really is up to the needs of your project.

432
00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:54.980
And the last thing that I do want to mention

433
00:19:54.980 --> 00:19:58.099
is that these are meant to be used in conjunction

434
00:19:58.099 --> 00:20:00.319
with world partition.

435
00:20:00.319 --> 00:20:02.579
And when you're using them,

436
00:20:02.619 --> 00:20:05.660
remember that they're meant to be used as modular blocks.

437
00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:08.660
So nesting is encouraged

438
00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:11.420
and will help you get the most out of this system.
